1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,812
This is Savitri. She sells tea in Gorakhpur. Every morning, before the city wakes, she lights her stove.

2
00:00:09,012 --> 00:00:14,661
By night, she has earned about ₹1,500. All cash. None of it written down.

3
00:00:14,661 --> 00:00:18,994
Eleven years, and this is how she has raised her family.

4
00:00:20,194 --> 00:00:26,241
Last monsoon, the rains came and did not stop. For days, almost nobody stopped for tea.

5
00:00:26,241 --> 00:00:31,316
The stock she had bought was wasted. The supplier still wanted his money.

6
00:00:32,516 --> 00:00:37,392
She went to the bank. They asked for proof of income. She had none.

7
00:00:37,392 --> 00:00:42,123
No statements, no history, nothing in writing. The answer was no.

8
00:00:42,123 --> 00:00:46,199
So, like millions, she borrowed from whoever would lend.

9
00:00:47,399 --> 00:00:52,286
Savitri is not alone. India has an estimated one crore street vendors.

10
00:00:52,286 --> 00:00:57,312
By one estimate, three in four small businesses rely on informal credit.

11
00:00:57,312 --> 00:01:01,081
PM SVANidhi has reached 69 lakh vendors, a real start.

12
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:07,503
But loans are small and fixed, and nothing protects a vendor when the rain takes her income.

13
00:01:08,703 --> 00:01:14,168
The gap is not willingness to repay. It is evidence. Banks cannot see her income.

14
00:01:14,168 --> 00:01:20,915
Insurers cannot see her loss. And a notebook, or an app anyone can edit, proves nothing to a lender.

15
00:01:22,115 --> 00:01:28,254
What if her voice was her paperwork? This is Kamai. She speaks her day in her own language.

16
00:01:28,254 --> 00:01:35,472
Kamai turns it into a record that cannot be quietly changed, one a lender can trust and an insurer can use.

17
00:01:36,672 --> 00:01:43,680
She signs up with her mobile number and a PIN, in Hindi or English. No app to learn. No forms to fill.

18
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:48,203
आज 1800 की बिक्री हुई और 1100 का माल लिया।
“Today I sold ₹1,800 and bought ₹1,100 of stock.”

19
00:01:48,503 --> 00:01:51,236
She says what she sold and what she bought.

20
00:01:51,236 --> 00:01:57,400
Kamai writes it down and seals every line to the one before, so history cannot be quietly edited.

21
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:02,684
Over weeks, a score builds from her own record: her earnings,

22
00:02:02,684 --> 00:02:07,103
how steady her weeks are, how much is verified, and any red flags.

23
00:02:07,103 --> 00:02:08,911
Nothing is hidden from her.

24
00:02:10,111 --> 00:02:12,363
The orange days are real rain-outs.

25
00:02:12,363 --> 00:02:17,895
Kamai reads real rainfall for her city, and sees her sales fall on the days it poured.

26
00:02:19,095 --> 00:02:24,606
The offer is sized on her weakest weeks, not her best. Thirty-three thousand rupees.

27
00:02:24,606 --> 00:02:29,460
One tap sends her summary to the lender. Nothing is shared until she taps.

28
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:36,498
The lender sees only people who applied. Every record is re-verified each time it is opened.

29
00:02:37,698 --> 00:02:40,259
Here, a vendor edited his past entries.

30
00:02:40,259 --> 00:02:46,957
The chain no longer matches, and the server refuses to approve him, even if the lender clicks approve.

31
00:02:48,157 --> 00:02:51,713
Another inflated his sales and re-signed the whole ledger.

32
00:02:51,713 --> 00:02:56,618
The cost check still catches him: his stock cost is far too low for a tea stall.

33
00:02:57,818 --> 00:03:03,307
And when the rains come? Her ledger plus the real rainfall record builds the claim.

34
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:09,325
No assessor, no bills. For Savitri, twelve rain-out days, about ninety-five hundred rupees.

35
00:03:10,525 --> 00:03:15,714
The insurer sees every claim, re-verified. Tampered ledgers are held, not paid.

36
00:03:15,714 --> 00:03:23,202
And we are honest about cost: this is the worst quarter, and the premium needed is far more than a vendor can pay.

37
00:03:23,202 --> 00:03:26,684
A pilot would fund it through the loan, or a subsidy.

38
00:03:27,884 --> 00:03:33,245
Here is what makes Kamai powerful. Her record cannot be quietly changed.

39
00:03:33,245 --> 00:03:38,755
Five fraud checks run on every book. Loans are sized on her weakest weeks.

40
00:03:38,755 --> 00:03:41,585
Claims follow her own verified losses.

41
00:03:41,585 --> 00:03:47,318
And cover raises what she can borrow: from ₹17,000 to ₹33,000, in our sample.

42
00:03:48,518 --> 00:03:53,092
Pieces of this exist. Ledger apps let vendors speak their entries.

43
00:03:53,092 --> 00:03:58,221
Pilots pay street vendors on rain and heat. PM SVANidhi gives small loans.

44
00:03:58,221 --> 00:04:02,726
We found no product that ties them together: a verifiable record,

45
00:04:02,726 --> 00:04:07,162
claims that follow her own loss, and cover that raises her loan.

46
00:04:07,162 --> 00:04:09,102
That is the gap Kamai fills.

47
00:04:10,302 --> 00:04:19,047
Today, this is a working product: real accounts, a real database, real rainfall, and rules enforced and tested on the server.

48
00:04:19,047 --> 00:04:24,993
The vendors in the demo are samples. We have not yet put it in front of real vendors.

49
00:04:26,193 --> 00:04:32,623
Next: a pilot with one lender and a few hundred vendors, to see if the score predicts repayment.

50
00:04:32,623 --> 00:04:37,445
Then bank data through an Account Aggregator, receipts read from photos,

51
00:04:37,445 --> 00:04:41,932
a licensed insurer, and WhatsApp and phone calls in more languages.

52
00:04:41,932 --> 00:04:44,209
So no one has to install anything.

53
00:04:45,409 --> 00:04:51,827
Savitri has worked eleven years without a single line on paper. Kamai gives her the first line.

54
00:04:51,827 --> 00:04:54,259
A passbook, for people banks ignore.

